WEBVTT

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.172
So...

00:00:02.192 --> 00:00:03.452
won't creating new money

00:00:03.472 --> 00:00:05.105
just cause inflation?

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:06.732
You've felt it at the checkout.

00:00:06.752 --> 00:00:08.132
The same basket of shopping

00:00:08.152 --> 00:00:10.172
costs more than it did a year ago.

00:00:10.192 --> 00:00:11.372
That's inflation...

00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:13.292
prices in general, going up.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:14.732
Not one price tag.

00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:16.172
The whole basket.

00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:17.879
To see what really drives it,

00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:19.462
go back to the shell factory.

00:00:20.912 --> 00:00:23.224
Britain, 1915.

00:00:23.354 --> 00:00:25.174
Unemployment, the share of workers

00:00:25.194 --> 00:00:27.974
without a job, is under one percent.

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:30.694
The war is paid for partly with new money.

00:00:30.714 --> 00:00:32.854
Submarines are sinking supply ships,

00:00:32.874 --> 00:00:35.254
and factories are turned over to war work.

00:00:35.274 --> 00:00:36.694
So more money is chasing

00:00:36.714 --> 00:00:38.161
fewer things to buy...

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:39.414
and by the end of the war,

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:40.494
prices have roughly

00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:41.984
doubled since it began.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.334
Now picture two factories.

00:00:45.384 --> 00:00:48.004
In the first, the machines stand idle,

00:00:48.024 --> 00:00:49.814
and workers wait at the gate.

00:00:49.944 --> 00:00:51.254
New money arrives...

00:00:51.304 --> 00:00:52.484
the workers go in,

00:00:52.504 --> 00:00:54.174
and more shells come out.

00:00:54.264 --> 00:00:56.054
Prices barely move.

00:00:56.184 --> 00:00:58.324
In the second, every machine and

00:00:58.344 --> 00:01:00.884
every worker is already flat out.

00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:02.404
The same money arrives...

00:01:02.424 --> 00:01:04.484
but no more shells can be made.

00:01:04.504 --> 00:01:06.324
So buyers bid against each other,

00:01:06.344 --> 00:01:08.004
and prices climb.

00:01:08.024 --> 00:01:09.284
Britain at war was close

00:01:09.304 --> 00:01:10.884
to the second factory...

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.694
short of workers, materials and machines.

00:01:14.944 --> 00:01:16.654
Homes work the same way.

00:01:16.784 --> 00:01:19.004
In 1915, workers poured

00:01:19.024 --> 00:01:20.844
into Glasgow for war work,

00:01:20.864 --> 00:01:22.657
and landlords pushed rents up...

00:01:22.677 --> 00:01:24.404
by as much as a quarter.

00:01:24.424 --> 00:01:26.644
By November, about twenty thousand

00:01:26.664 --> 00:01:29.431
households were refusing to pay the rise.

00:01:29.451 --> 00:01:32.177
Within weeks, Parliament froze rents.

00:01:32.197 --> 00:01:34.134
Homes can't be built in a hurry.

00:01:35.484 --> 00:01:36.694
So test it.

00:01:36.824 --> 00:01:38.894
Japan, twenty thirteen.

00:01:38.984 --> 00:01:40.084
Its central bank,

00:01:40.104 --> 00:01:41.814
the bank that creates the yen,

00:01:41.864 --> 00:01:43.284
sets out to double the money

00:01:43.304 --> 00:01:45.791
it has created, in two years.

00:01:45.811 --> 00:01:47.044
If new money on its own

00:01:47.064 --> 00:01:49.364
pushed prices up, they'd soar.

00:01:49.384 --> 00:01:51.737
For eight years, they barely move...

00:01:51.757 --> 00:01:53.524
about half a percent a year.

00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:56.364
After the 2008 crash, the Bank of England,

00:01:56.384 --> 00:01:57.524
Britain's central bank,

00:01:57.544 --> 00:02:00.324
creates hundreds of billions of pounds.

00:02:00.344 --> 00:02:01.364
Its own study finds

00:02:01.384 --> 00:02:02.484
the new money pushed up

00:02:02.504 --> 00:02:03.604
the prices of shares

00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:04.857
and other investments...

00:02:04.877 --> 00:02:05.924
and the gains went to

00:02:05.944 --> 00:02:07.454
whoever owned the most.

00:02:08.704 --> 00:02:11.014
Germany, 1923.

00:02:11.144 --> 00:02:13.374
Prices are already racing.

00:02:13.464 --> 00:02:14.884
Then French and Belgian

00:02:14.904 --> 00:02:16.804
soldiers march into the Ruhr,

00:02:16.824 --> 00:02:19.164
Germany's coal and steel heartland.

00:02:19.184 --> 00:02:20.404
The government tells workers

00:02:20.424 --> 00:02:22.191
to stop work in protest...

00:02:22.211 --> 00:02:24.564
and keeps paying them, with new money.

00:02:24.584 --> 00:02:26.884
By November, one American dollar costs

00:02:26.904 --> 00:02:29.684
more than four trillion German marks.

00:02:29.704 --> 00:02:30.404
This time,

00:02:30.424 --> 00:02:32.964
new money really did push prices up...

00:02:32.984 --> 00:02:34.974
while less and less was being made.

00:02:36.224 --> 00:02:38.224
Late 1973.

00:02:38.354 --> 00:02:39.454
Prices are already

00:02:39.474 --> 00:02:40.654
climbing when the price of

00:02:40.674 --> 00:02:42.334
oil nearly quadruples,

00:02:42.354 --> 00:02:43.747
in about three months...

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:45.374
and almost everything made or

00:02:45.394 --> 00:02:47.667
moved with fuel gets dearer.

00:02:47.687 --> 00:02:50.094
So sometimes the cause isn't spending.

00:02:50.114 --> 00:02:51.321
It's costs.

00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:52.147
And here?

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:54.254
The latest figures say prices rose four

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:56.574
point one percent in the year to June.

00:02:56.594 --> 00:02:57.667
Leave fuel out...

00:02:57.687 --> 00:02:59.224
and it's two point nine.

00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:01.894
So yes...

00:03:02.024 --> 00:03:04.164
new money can push prices up,

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:06.004
when it's spent where workers and machines

00:03:06.024 --> 00:03:07.494
are already flat out,

00:03:07.544 --> 00:03:08.764
or on things we can't

00:03:08.784 --> 00:03:10.334
make more of in a hurry.

00:03:10.504 --> 00:03:12.644
In twenty twenty-one, our unemployment

00:03:12.664 --> 00:03:15.204
hit its lowest since records began.

00:03:15.224 --> 00:03:16.804
House prices rose about thirty

00:03:16.824 --> 00:03:18.644
percent in twelve months.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:19.964
An independent review of the

00:03:19.984 --> 00:03:21.444
Reserve Bank says the support

00:03:21.464 --> 00:03:22.804
from the bank and the government

00:03:22.824 --> 00:03:24.964
together was right at first...

00:03:24.984 --> 00:03:27.294
but too strong, for too long.

00:03:28.644 --> 00:03:30.904
So what's the usual cure?

00:03:31.074 --> 00:03:33.094
Britain, 1920.

00:03:33.114 --> 00:03:34.014
The Bank of England

00:03:34.034 --> 00:03:35.534
pushes its interest rate,

00:03:35.554 --> 00:03:36.894
the price of borrowing,

00:03:36.914 --> 00:03:38.654
up to seven percent.

00:03:38.674 --> 00:03:41.574
Loans cost more, and people spend less.

00:03:41.594 --> 00:03:43.704
Within a year, unemployment jumps from

00:03:43.754 --> 00:03:46.344
about two percent to eleven percent.

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:47.774
Prices do fall...

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:49.774
but people pay with their jobs.

00:03:49.794 --> 00:03:52.374
Mattei argues that was part of the point.

00:03:52.394 --> 00:03:54.654
With fewer jobs to go round, she says,

00:03:54.674 --> 00:03:57.304
workers lose the power to ask for more.

00:03:58.754 --> 00:04:01.944
Here, our central bank, the Reserve Bank,

00:04:01.964 --> 00:04:03.664
pushed its interest rate from a quarter

00:04:03.684 --> 00:04:06.064
of a percent to five and a half percent by

00:04:06.084 --> 00:04:09.024
twenty twenty-three, to bring prices down.

00:04:09.044 --> 00:04:10.891
Since the end of twenty twenty-one,

00:04:10.911 --> 00:04:11.744
unemployment has

00:04:11.764 --> 00:04:13.104
climbed from three point two

00:04:13.124 --> 00:04:15.371
percent to five point six.

00:04:15.391 --> 00:04:17.024
And this year, the Reserve Bank

00:04:17.044 --> 00:04:19.451
is raising its interest rate again.

00:04:19.471 --> 00:04:20.944
So next time someone tells you

00:04:20.964 --> 00:04:22.944
new money causes inflation...

00:04:22.964 --> 00:04:23.824
ask them.

00:04:23.844 --> 00:04:24.971
Spent on what?

00:04:24.991 --> 00:04:26.474
And who pays to stop it?

